### I CANNOT SOLVE THIS

The Twin Prime Conjecture — the assertion that there are infinitely many primes $p$ such that $p+2$ is also prime — is one of the most famous **open problems** in number theory. It is listed among Landau's problems (the fourth), and the problem statement itself is tagged `@[category research open, AMS 11]`, confirming its status as an unsolved research problem.

No proof is currently known. The best unconditional results to date (Zhang 2013, Maynard 2015, the Polymath project) establish that there are infinitely many prime pairs with bounded gap $H$ (currently $H \le 246$), but reducing this bound to $H = 2$ — the twin prime case — remains out of reach. Under strong conjectures such as the Hardy–Littlewood prime-pair conjecture, the asymptotic count $\pi_2(x) \sim 2C_2 \int_2^x \frac{dt}{(\ln t)^2}$ (with $C_2$ the twin prime constant) would follow, but these too are unproven.

Since this is an open problem with no known proof, I cannot provide one.

